    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • XPENG Reveals G9L at the 2026 Paris Motor Show, Dubbed As the Ultimate \"Large SUV for the AI Era\"\nRenault Built Its Most Expensive Car Ever and Already Got Over 1,000 Orders for It\n13 photos\nPhoto: Renault\nRenault has officially secured over 1,100 orders for the Renault 5 Turbo 3E. Despite the jaw-dropping price, which makes it the most expensive production Renault ever, demand for the all-electric drift missile has been exceptionally strong since the moment reservations opened.\nThe first batch of 700 allocations, comprising 500 customer spots and 200 dealer network spots, sold out in a matter of hours. Renault brand CEO Fabrice Cambolive confirmed that more than half of the total production run of 1,980 units is already spoken for.\nBuyers from Europe, Japan, the Middle East, and Australia have placed deposits to lock in their build slots. Renault estimates that the remaining allocations will sell out before customer deliveries officially begin in early 2028.\nThe Renault 5 Turbo 3E is the most expensive production car ever built by the French automaker. It completely shatters any record, with a staggering starting price of 155,000 euros, which is the equivalent of roughly $173,700 for the first 500 units. Following the initial launch window, the official base price transitions to 160,000 euros ($179,311).\nPhoto: Renault Prior to the arrival of the 5 Turbo 3E, the most expensive production Renault was the track-focused Megane RS Trophy-R , which started at around 96,000 euros ($107,660). However, due to the extensive customization possibilities, Renault Group CEO Francois Provost noted that heavily personalized builds are expected to climb to over 350,000 euros ($392,500).\nBut to get there, prospective buyers must first complete a dedicated priority interest form through the official Renault platform. Once the enquiry is approved, Renault matches the buyer with a local dealership to arrange a formal, in-person consultation. To officially secure a build slot, customers must first secure a build slot by wiring a 50,000-euro deposit, which will be fully credited toward the final balance, set to be invoiced upon configuration.\nBecause only 1,980 vehicles will see the light of day, early customers are granted a priority perk: they will have the option to pay a premium to pick their car’s chassis production number, a move that is subject to availability.\nThe customization program won't start before mid-2027. Future owners will work directly with Renault designers. The option list includes extreme retro-neon racing liveries, classic heritage color schemes, like the iconic 1980s Rouge Grenade or the 1982 Tour de Corse yellow-and-black rally look. Deliveries are scheduled to begin in early 2028.\nPhoto: Renault Florent Ingargiola, the car's product leader confirmed the over 1,000 orders in a conversation with Piston Heads.\nWhat are customers getting for the money? The Renault-badged pocket rocket is powered by a dual-motor powertrain, with both motors mounted in the back, powering each rear wheel independently. The setup produces 547 horsepower (555 metric horsepower) and 516 pound-feet (700 Newton meters) of torque.\nIt can flash from 0 to 62 mph (0 to 100 kph) in 3.5 seconds, hit 124 mph (200 kph) in 9.0 seconds, and max out at 167 mph (270 kph). The car is sitting on an 800-volt architecture and is equipped with a 70-kWh battery pack, which can store enough energy for a drive of up to 248 miles (400 kilometers) before it needs to be plugged in again.\nThe Renault 5 Turbo 3E measures 160.6 inches (4,080 millimeters) in length, 79.9 inches (2,030 millimeters) in width, and 54.3 inches (1,380 millimeters) in height, sitting on a wheelbase of 101.2 inches (2,570 millimeters).\nWith that sort of price and that sort of oomph, the 5 Turbo 3E is in a league of ts own with no rival in the market today or any time soon.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nrenault 5 turbo 3E Renault Renault 5 Turbo 3E horsepower Renault 5 Turbo 3E price most expensive Renault\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1970 \"Chevelle SS\" 454 Wagon Is an Eight-People Muscle Car, Looks and Drives Like a 400-HP Dream",
